speechtext:
If I could use the words to a minister, I would say, what nonsense. It provided for expenses. I have under my hand a speech made by the Minister of Agriculture (Mr. Gardiner) in Saskatchewan in which he said that the wheat act of last session was the act of a Liberal authority and not the Conservative party, that it embodied what they wanted and not what the Conservative government wanted. I read yesterday from section 7 (f), which was agreed to by the committee and accepted unanimously by the house. That section provides that at least something must be paid as there was some value attached to wheat, despite what has been said to the contrary. The section reads:
(f) notwithstanding anything hereinbefore contained, to acquire from Canadian Cooperative Wheat Producers, Limited, upon terms to be approved by the governor in council, all wheat or contracts to purchase or take delivery of wheat in respect of which the government of Canada has given a guarantee;
The method is approved by statute. I am going to point out that this statute has no place in this parliament. When parliament has provided a way of dealing with a matter, that way must be pursued or the statute must be repealed. As long as this statute stands, until it is repealed, the method under which the matter is to be dealt with is not a method devised by the late government, not a method devised by a government, but a method approved of by this house as a whole; and it was in pursuance of that that this approval was being given. Now it is suggested that it must have had some political significance. I ask you, Mr. Chairman, in view of the observations made by the minister this afternoon, whether he thinks that on the J 0th day of October any minister who was desirous of using it for political purposes would have added a provision such as has been suggested, namely, that there must be a verification and certificates produced by auditors before any payments could be made.
